.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt DTFL_STREAM_AGGREGATE 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_stream_aggregate
.Nd fold keyed values within each timestamp
.Sh LIBRARY
Timely and differential dataflow library
.Pq libdtfl , \-ldtfl
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/timely_ops.h
.Ft dtfl_stream *
.Fo dtfl_stream_aggregate
.Fa "dtfl_stream *st"
.Fa "size_t agg_size"
.Fa "dtfl_agg_fold_fn fold"
.Fa "dtfl_agg_emit_fn emit"
.Fa "const dtfl_type *out_type"
.Fa "dtfl_ctx ctx"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn dtfl_stream_aggregate
folds the values of
.Pq key , value
records within each timestamp, keyed by the key, then emits one result
per key once the timestamp is complete.
The records of
.Fa st
are pairs whose first field is the key; the input is exchanged by the
key's hash, so all records with the same key reach the same worker.
.Pp
Each distinct key within a time keeps an aggregate of
.Fa agg_size
bytes, zero-initialised on first sight.
.Fn fold
is called for each
.Pq key , value
with the key and value bytes and the aggregate to update.
Once the input frontier passes the time,
.Fn emit
is called once per key with the key and the final aggregate and writes
the result records of
.Fa out_type
through its
.Vt dtfl_emit
handle.
The context
.Fa ctx
is released by its destructor when the operator is dropped (PLAN D5).
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn dtfl_stream_aggregate
returns the stream of emitted result records, carrying
.Fa out_type .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* Sum values per key % 2 within a time. */
static void sum(void *c, const void *k, size_t kl,
    const void *v, size_t vl, void *agg)
	{ *(uint64_t *)agg += dtfl_get_u64(v); }
dtfl_stream *out = dtfl_stream_aggregate(s, sizeof(uint64_t),
    sum, emit, pair_type, DTFL_NOCTX);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_stream_count 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_stream_state_machine 3 ,
.Xr dtfl 7
.Sh HISTORY
This is the C form of timely's
.Vt Aggregate
trait.
It is public at the pinned release
.Pq Li timely-v0.31.0
and was removed upstream afterward (#818); the port keeps the pinned
behavior.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
The streams and the operator belong to the thread that runs the worker
and must not be used from another thread (PLAN section 5).
